/** \page qso_buttons Control buttons for Dashboard
\section description_15 Description

This pane is a highly configured pane that provides the control buttons that are 
relevant to the current state of the Dashboard.
 
\image html qso_buttons_1.png "Position of control buttons within dashboard window"
\image latex qso_buttons_1.png "Position of control buttons within dashboard window"

Only those buttons that are effective in the current state of the Dashboard are shown 
and some may be inactive for other reasons.

\section features_1 Features

The complete list of buttons:

- <B>Add Call</B> Add a new QSO record to the net in the editor view.
- <B>Add QSO</B> Create a new QSO record without initialising time and rig data.
- <B>Browse Log</B> Open the QSO browser (rather than editor) displaying the current QSO.
The browser allows all fields to be displayed rather than the limited selection in the editor.
- <B>B4?</B> Extract all QSOs with the callsign in the selected QSO.
- <B>Cancel</B> Cancel the current viewing or editing of the QSO record.
- <B>Cancel Query</B> Cancel the query.
- <B>Check</B> Start the search for the above data.
- <B>Clone QSO</B> Create a new QSO record copying rig data.
- <B>Copy QSO</B> Create a new QSO record copying call and rig data.
- <B>Delete QSO</B> Remove the selected QSO from the log.
- <B>Done</B> Keep the result of any merge action.
- <B>DX?</B> Display the parsing of the callsign in the selected QSO record.
- <B>Edit Net</B> Open all records that could form a net (same frequency, close time) in the editor.
- <B>Edit QSO</B> Opens the QSO record in edit mode.
- <B>End Session</B> End the current logging session. This will close any open QSO records and stop any rig data being read.
- <B>Keep 1</B> <I>(Dupe check)</I> Keep the left hand record.
- <B>Keep 1 & 2</B> <I>(Dupe check)</I> keep both records. They are not duplicates.
- <B>Keep 2</B> <I>(Dupe check)</I> Keep the right hand record.
- <B>Log</B> Log the QSO being entered or edited. If the QSO hadn't been started, start it first. 
If the QSO is being entered, then set the end time.
- <B>Merge</B> <I>(Dupe check)</I> Merge data between the two records.
- <B>Merge QSO</B> <I>(Query Mode)</I> Merge data between the queried record and the target record.
- <B>Parse QSO</B> Add DXCC etc detils to the current record.
- <B>Query</B> Open an editor view to allow details for a search to be entered.
- <B>Reject QSO</B> <I>(Query Mode)</I> Do not add the queried QSO record to the log.
- <B>Replace CAT</B> Replace the rig data in the selected record with data freshly read from the rig.
- <B>Restart</B> Cancel the current QSO entry and start a new one.
- <B>Save</B> Log the QSO being entered or edited. If the QSO hadn't been started, start it first. 
- <B>Save & Edit</B> Log the QSO (as above), but continue with the QSO record open in edit mode.
- <B>Save & New</B> Save the QSO (as above) and start a new QSO.
- <B>Save & View</B> Log the QSO (as above), but continue with the QSO record in read-only mode.
- <B>Save Net</B> Save all records in the editor view.
- <B>Start Net</B> Start a QSO, expecting it to be the first of a (say club) net.
- <B>Start QSO</B> Start a real-time (on-air) QSO. This fixes the QSO start date and time.
as well as data read from the rig.
- <B>Start Session</B> Make the dashboard ready to start logging QSOs.
- <B>Test Import</B> <I>(Query mode)</I> Start a search to match import record in log.
- <B>Update CAT</B> Copy data from the rig if none exist in the selected record.
- <B>U/d Station</B> Update the record using the current Location, Operator and Station Callsign.
- <B>View QSO</B> Opens the QSO record in read-only mode.
- <B>[Left arrow]</B> Select the previous record.
- <B>[Left arrow and bar]</B> Select the first record - either in the current log extract or in a net edit.
- <B>[Reading glass]</B> Search for a possible match for the query record.
- <B>[Reading glass] ALL.TXT</B> Search the WSJT-X ALL.TXT for evidence of a QSO with the query record.
- <B>[Reading glass] QRZ.com</B> Open the QRZ.com page for the callsign in the selected record.
- <B>[Right arrow]</B> Select the next record.
- <B>[Right arrow and bar]</B> Select the last record.

See \ref shortcuts for keyboard shortcuts that can be used to perform these actions.

\page shortcuts Keyboard shortcuts

The following shortcuts are available from most positions in the QSO manager window:

- <B>ALT a</B> Add QSO
- <B>ALT b</B> Abandon QSO
- <B>ALT c</B> Add Call (to net)
- <B>ALT d</B> End Session
- <B>ALT e</B> Edit QSO
- <B>ALT g</B> Log Net
- <B>ALT l</B> Log QSO
- <B>ALT n</B> Start Net
- <B>ALT q</B> Start QSO
- <B>ALT s</B> Start Session
- <B>ALT t</B> Edit Net
- <B>ALT u</B> Quit Net
- <B>ALT x</B> Delete QSO
- <B>ALT LEFT</B> Previous Entry
- <B>ALT SHIFT LEFT</B> First Entry
- <B>ALT RIGHT</B> Next Entry
- <B>ALT SHIFT RIGHT</B> Last Entry

The following shortcuts are available from any of the windows.

- <B>ALT 1..9</B> Switches active rig to the one indexed 1..9 in the displayed rigs.
- <B>CTRL w</B> Switches between all shown (ie visible or iconified) windows.
- <B>Escape</B> Closes the application.
- <B>F1</B> Opens the user guide. If possible, the user guide will open
at a page relevant to the component currently with focus, otherwise it
will open at the front page.

*/